{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued corrections to several articles published on August 25th. These corrections address inaccuracies ranging from geographical scope to personal details and historical facts. The paper is committed to accuracy and has promptly rectified errors identified in its reporting. The corrections highlight the importance of meticulous fact-checking in journalism. The issues cover a variety of topics, demonstrating the breadth of the Guardian’s coverage.\n\n***\n\nSection 2\nOne correction clarified the location of a new pet theft law, specifying it applies to England and Northern Ireland, not the entire UK. Another corrected a reference to Eileen Burbidge, the executive director of Fertifa, clarifying she is not the founder, whose titles are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan. A third correction addressed a misattribution of a work with director Alex Lawther, correcting the actor Juliet Stevenson’s reference to him. Furthermore, the family in the film *Bend It Like Beckham* are Sikh, not Muslim, as previously stated.\n\nSection 3\n* The article regarding the pet theft law had incorrectly implied a UK-wide application when it only applies to England and Northern Ireland.\n*  Information about the founders of Fertifa was inaccurate, leading to a misrepresentation of Eileen Burbidge’s role within the company. \n\nSection 4\nThe corrections also included an error regarding Venetia Stanley’s father's religious conversion; it was her uncle, not her father, who converted to Islam. Additionally, a correction was made regarding the family background of the characters in *Bend It Like Beckham*. Other recently amended articles cover a range of topics, including the arrest of Telegram app founder Pavel Durov, the role of royal servants, and the future of the British economy.